{
  "summary": "Hardens node implementations of interfaces::Chain against crashes caused by invalid arguments or IPC notification failures. Replaces asserts and null dereferences in getBlockHash, broadcastTransaction, and checkChainLimits with bounds checks and error returns. Wraps validation notification forwarding in NotificationsProxy to catch ipc::Exception and avoid crashing the validation scheduler thread, and fixes findAncestorByHash returning true when the descendant block hash is unknown.",
  "problem": "interfaces::Chain methods were designed for well-behaved in-process callers like the internal wallet and assert or dereference null on invalid inputs. When exposed across IPC to out-of-process clients, omitted parameters or invalid inputs crash bitcoin-node, and errors returned by client notification handlers terminate the validation interface scheduler thread. Additionally, findAncestorByHash falsely reports success when the descendant block is missing.",
  "evidence": [
    "Fixes #36472 ('interfaces: findAncestorByHash returns true for an unknown descendant')",
    "Author notes: 'The problems were reported by zaidmstrr, willcl-ark and xyzconstant while reviewing #29409, and by enirox001 in #36472.'",
    "Commit message notes that notifications run on the validation interface scheduler thread, where uncaught exceptions from disconnected or failing IPC clients terminate the node process."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "#29409"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "This PR hardens Bitcoin Core's Chain interface implementations against crashes from invalid arguments and IPC client errors. Currently, Chain methods assume well-behaved in-process callers, asserting or dereferencing null pointers on invalid inputs and permitting client exceptions on the validation scheduler thread to terminate the node. It also fixes a logic bug where findAncestorByHash reports success for unknown descendant blocks (issue 36472). While standalone, these changes primarily safeguard the node before exposing the Chain interface over multiprocess IPC in PR 29409.",
